{"schema_version":1,"task_id":"theory-43d8de3eae5f57c6","paper_id":"2510.27497v1","variant":"original","kind":"theory","outcome":"checked","reason":null,"error":null,"findings":[{"finding_id":"theory-43d8de3eae5f57c6-f0","task_id":"theory-43d8de3eae5f57c6","paper_id":"2510.27497v1","variant":"original","error_class":"math_error","anchor":{"kind":"sentence","sentence_id":"s302","start":0,"end":88},"target":{"kind":"setting","key":"math"},"summary":"Theorem 2: Theorem 2 incorrectly states that the fourth point should be coplanar with the three basis vectors. To resolve sign and reflection ambiguities of the three orthogonal axes, the fourth point must not lie on any coordinate plane, i.e., it must be non-coplanar with the coordinate planes, having non-zero coordinates along all three axes, as correctly noted in Theorem 1 and later in the proo","explanation":"high confidence. Theorem 2 incorrectly states that the fourth point should be coplanar with the three basis vectors. To resolve sign and reflection ambiguities of the three orthogonal axes, the fourth point must not lie on any coordinate plane, i.e., it must be non-coplanar with the coordinate planes, having non-zero coordinates along all three axes, as correctly noted in Theorem 1 and later in the proof in sentence s316 as nonplanar to the three base axes. A point coplanar with the coordinate planes would have a zero coordinate, failing to determine the sign of the corresponding axis. Witness: non-coplanar with the coordinate planes","evidence":[{"kind":"quote","source":{"kind":"submission","id":"2510.27497v1","title":null,"url":null},"text":"Then we need to incorporate a fourth point that is coplanar with the three basis vectors","page":null,"char_start":0,"char_end":88,"path":null,"line_start":null,"line_end":null}],"uncertain":false}],"attempts":1}
